Well, no, there are a couple of issues that
are very much "C" issues.

The first issue is that some C programmer
might make inappropriate assumptions about
what that flag implies, and conditionally
compile code under it whose variations have
nothing to do with that OS, and everything to
do with that programmer's misunderstanding of
the flag.

Like most subtle bugs, you might be the first
one whose compilation finds the case where the
misuse causes a visible problem.

The second issue is that conditional compilation
of code snippet by snippet based on flags in the
source is very much a C-associated feature, and
isn't the only way to achieve the desired result.

Other ways to do that are used in other languages,
like controls at link edit time that bring in
whole separately written and compiled modules in
which the platform dependencies have been carefully
isolated, like doing the decisions at run time with
manifest constants changed for each build or even
with information detected from the environment.

The C way _works_, but it is immensely unrobust,
and in my use of C since 1983, usually a
maintenance nightmare for multiplatform code.

Thus the comment. Now that computers have finally
gotten fast enough for something platform independent
like Java to be feasible, the feeling of relief is
frankly wonderful, and my ability to maintain even
my own code has dramatically improved.

